---
title: "Cards on hover"
---

A long document is full of references that point elsewhere: "see @fig-flow", "as
@sec-why explains". Following one means jumping away and losing your place. Hovering a
reference here pops a small card that renders the *target itself*, drawn straight from
the page by its content-hash id, so you can glance without leaving.

## Why it works {#sec-why}

Every block carries a stable id, so the card shows the actual target, not a guessed
snippet. A heading shows itself plus its first lines; a figure shows the figure.

A footnote[^aside] is the exception, and for the reason the whole feature exists: its
note is already in the margin beside the line that cites it, so there is no jump to
save the reader from. Previewing it would cover the page to show text a few centimetres
to the right.

See @sec-why above, or the diagram in @fig-flow below.

```{mermaid}
%%| label: fig-flow
%%| fig-cap: "Hover a reference to preview its target."
flowchart LR
  Reference -->|hover| Card
  Card -->|renders| Target
```

The card is read-only and reuses what the renderer already computed, so it costs nothing
extra at build time.

[^aside]: This note is deliberately *not* a hover target: it is already visible beside
the paragraph that cites it.
